00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:36.989
As Jack mentioned, right now I'm at Georgia
Institute of Technology, but I was at Ohio

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:41.269
State for 15 years at the Department of Biomedical
Informatics.

00:02:41.269 --> 00:02:46.049
I was one of the founding faculty members
who started the department, and just before

00:02:46.049 --> 00:02:51.239
I moved here I was one of the co-directors
of MIDAs multi-modeling and Integrative Data

00:02:51.239 --> 00:02:54.180
Analytics Training Program.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.079
So I am always motivated by biomedical applications.

00:02:58.079 --> 00:03:03.569
I'm a computer scientist at heart, so this
talk will be, as Jack mentioned, from the

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:06.209
more computational perspective.

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:12.639
And I will try to give you a brief introduction,
a quick little crash course on computer architecture

00:03:12.639 --> 00:03:15.650
in general high performance computing architectures.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:21.029
I need to provide some background information
so that we are all on the same page.

00:03:21.029 --> 00:03:29.629
Then, I will focus on three, I will say, golden
rules that when you're making your application

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:34.680
faster on high performance computing or today's
computing environment.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.829
And I will give some simple examples there,
and I will finish up with one application

00:03:39.829 --> 00:03:45.370
that shows many of those techniques to get
a [? combine ?]

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:48.129
So let's start with high performance computing.

00:03:48.129 --> 00:03:51.229
What do we really mean by high performance
computing?

00:03:51.229 --> 00:03:57.189
It's one of those terms that there's really
no agreed upon terminology.

00:03:57.189 --> 00:04:02.079
One can say that there's no such thing as
low performance computing, so all of the computing

00:04:02.079 --> 00:04:03.739
has to be high performance.

00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:08.260
Why one would want to compute anything slow,
you want to compute everything fast, which

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:11.340
I couldn't agree more.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:16.180
Couple definitions from a couple of different
places, I don't want to read all of these,

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:26.760
but what I want to point out that many of
those definitions really tells that aggregating

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.169
computing power to solve bigger problem.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:37.560
And it talks about a need for high bandwidth
[? NIS ?] network and high computing capabilities.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:43.030
But the definition I would like to use is
something a little bit simpler.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:49.590
I just want to use available computing platforms
to run my applications quickly, and I want

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:52.080
to do this very efficiently.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.350
So for me, that's what high performance computing
is.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:05:00.580
It doesn't always mean supercomputing, but
I'll come to that in a minute.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:02.939
So why do we care about speed?

00:05:02.939 --> 00:05:10.580
Well, clearly, there are many reasons and
there is no point in laboring the point.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.680
We don't want science to wait for computing,
we want to compute fast so that we can make

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:19.300
the scientific advancement faster.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:26.450
But what I would like to emphasize is we really
need to care about efficiency in computing.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:33.409
So when we make computation efficient-- that
means if we can leverage the computational

00:05:33.409 --> 00:05:39.280
resources much better --that means there will
be more resources available to all of us.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.050
So you can do more computations so efficiency
is important.

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:52.169
One thing that's sometimes overlooked is also,
someone has to pay the bills.

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:56.770
So if you're doing a computation say in a
cloud computing environment, if you only care

00:05:56.770 --> 00:06:02.720
about getting it fast and if you're using
thousands of processors, are you going to

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:04.699
pay for thousands of processors?

00:06:04.699 --> 00:06:11.669
But if you can do it efficiently on less number
of processors, that will be also very fast

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:12.669
as well.

00:06:12.669 --> 00:06:17.330
So I can prove that, but I don't really have
time to do that, so I just want to emphasize

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:24.389
that whenever you are looking for application,
as much as you want it to run it fast, you

00:06:24.389 --> 00:06:26.789
really want to be efficient as well.

00:06:26.789 --> 00:06:30.560
So supercomputing is definitely HPC.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.580
But in my definition, HPC doesn't just mean
supercomputing.

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:36.729
We will look at some supercomputers.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:44.170
Not all of us will have access to those kind
of supercomputers, but I want to tell you

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:45.759
a few
things about high performance computing and

00:06:45.759 --> 00:06:51.419
architecture so that you can use in whatever
the environments you have.

00:06:51.419 --> 00:06:55.029
So let me start with computing today.

00:06:55.029 --> 00:07:00.060
So I'm going to argue that computing today
really means parallel computing, and you need

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:02.440
to do high performance computing.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:03.949
Why is that?

00:07:03.949 --> 00:07:09.189
Well, first of all, any computer you touch
today has parallel processing power.

00:07:09.189 --> 00:07:13.909
And I will start with a very simple example,
your laptop probably has at least two cores.

00:07:13.909 --> 00:07:20.009
Inside the CPU, it has two cores that can
do two things at the same time.

00:07:20.009 --> 00:07:27.680
And even your cell phone will have 4 to 8
cores today.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:37.419
So that means that any computer you see will
have multiple processing power elements.

00:07:37.419 --> 00:07:40.629
So it's there so we have to use them.

00:07:40.629 --> 00:07:46.000
The second rationale is well, this is BD2K
7, we're talking about big data and science

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.349
and hence the computational data need is really
big.

00:07:50.349 --> 00:07:57.719
We really need to solve some problems that
today, you cannot fit into whatever your local

00:07:57.719 --> 00:08:00.150
computer, your desktop computer, is.

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:06.560
So some examples are clearly-- includes a
genomic applications for example, method genomics

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:08.099
is one case.

00:08:08.099 --> 00:08:14.400
If you want to solve method genomics code
problem, you will not be able to run most

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:15.930
likely in your laptop.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:21.249
You will need some machine that has much bigger
memory.

00:08:21.249 --> 00:08:25.819
And the other thing is in some cases, you
may fit into your computer, but it may be

00:08:25.819 --> 00:08:27.509
taking too long.

00:08:27.509 --> 00:08:29.719
So we don't really have time to do this.

00:08:29.719 --> 00:08:34.279
So that's why we need to take into account
parallelism, and we need to make it efficient.

00:08:34.279 --> 00:08:36.860
So basically, three reasons.

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:44.130
One is well, it's there, all the computations,
resources today has parallelism, so if you're

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:47.490
not using them, you're wasting those resources.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:54.230
The second is your problem doesn't fit in
your small computer so you have to do it parallel.

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:59.079
The third is it takes too long and you want
to do it fast.

00:08:59.079 --> 00:09:05.810
And just to show that tsunami of data, these
are some slides that you probably have seen

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:08.690
a few times already.

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:16.670
So just sequences and whole genome sequencing
data in [? Janbang ?] is really exponentially

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:19.400
growing, and we have more than petabyte of
data.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:26.029
And we are getting some technologies like
Oxford Nanopores, which are small and will

00:09:26.029 --> 00:09:30.240
make even generational to data much faster.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:37.860
So now I want to switch gears and go into
a little bit computer architecture.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:45.529
So this is a chart of the performance of a
single processor over the time starting from

00:09:45.529 --> 00:09:54.139
early 1978 to 2012 or so, but the trend is
very similar after this point.

00:09:54.139 --> 00:10:01.010
What I want to highlight here is if you're
doing science between mid-'80s and 2003, if

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:05.320
you want to make your code faster, all you
had to do was just wait.

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:10.209
If you wait 18 months, you'll get a faster
processor almost twice as fast and your code

00:10:10.209 --> 00:10:11.519
will run fast.

00:10:11.519 --> 00:10:24.240
But around 2003, we actually hit a few technological
barriers that computer architects had to change

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:27.209
the way that we produce processors.

00:10:27.209 --> 00:10:32.529
And this is where we start having multicore
processors.

00:10:32.529 --> 00:10:39.480
From that point on, if you want to get your
code faster you cannot just rely on time and

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:45.600
simple technology advancements that will make
your existing sequential code run fast.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.190
You really have to think about parallelism.

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:48.190
sequential code run fast.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:49.190
You really have to think about parallelism.

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:53.980
One thing I really want to highlight here
when we talk about speed and we're measuring

00:10:53.980 --> 00:11:02.459
the performance of the program, we usually
use two terms, bandwidth and latency, or throughput

00:11:02.459 --> 00:11:05.550
or response time similarly means the same
thing.

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:11.390
The bandwidth is the total work done in a
given time.

00:11:11.390 --> 00:11:16.810
So if you are, for example, doing sequence
mapping, you have input reads.

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:22.990
How many of those reads you can map in a second,
this is your bandwidth.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:29.580
The latency is the time between start and
completion of the event.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:37.240
So in that case, the latency of mapping a
single read is the time it takes really finding

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:41.420
that in the whole genome pointing in that
location.

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:49.000
As you can guess from these problem description,
increasing bandwidth or throughput is much

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:50.000
easier actually.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:54.600
You control more resources and you can get
to higher bandwidth or higher throughput.

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:59.649
But reducing the latency, getting faster response
time is not that easy.

00:11:59.649 --> 00:12:03.290
And you will see that in computer architecture
as well.

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:10.430
If you plot the relative latency improvement
over the bandwidth improvements-- against

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:15.470
the bandwidth improvement, you'll see that
all of the computer technology, including

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:21.269
processors, network disk, the bandwidth's
really improved much higher than latency.

00:12:21.269 --> 00:12:22.350
What does it mean?

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:29.740
We're really adding more type of resources,
so you really need to take advantage of those

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:32.970
parallelism in your code to be able to make
it fast.

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:33.970
OK.

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:38.370
Now, I'm going to talk about, really, computer
architectures.

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:46.450
And I'm going to use Flynn's taxonomy on how
we classify the computer architectures.

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:55.410
So in one axis, we are looking at how many
instructions we can run at any point in time.

00:12:55.410 --> 00:13:00.370
And we will just classify them as, I can run
a single instruction or I can run multiple

00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:02.550
instruction with any point in time.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:06.670
And in the other axis, we'll look at the data.

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:11.360
How many data element I can process at any
time?

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:13.900
It will be either single or multiple.

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:20.260
So on the upper left corner is single instruction,
single data corner, which is your sequential

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:22.079
computer.

00:13:22.079 --> 00:13:30.540
So, basically, you can process one data at
any given time.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:35.420
There are alternative architectures where
you can actually do process multiple data

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:42.180
elements, but operate and do the same instruction.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:45.329
Let me just show them a picture of it.

00:13:45.329 --> 00:13:48.180
So this is your single instruction, single
data.

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:50.990
Basically, your standard CPU.

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:57.810
So you have input data coming and your processor
is doing one instruction at a time and producing

00:13:57.810 --> 00:13:59.839
the outputs.

00:13:59.839 --> 00:14:01.649
That's your basic sequential code.

00:14:01.649 --> 00:14:11.790
I'll turn it the way of you have to do a repetitive
task that you can actually do the same.

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:15.140
You will do the same processing in multiple
elements.

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:20.879
Instead of doing them one by one, you can
process them in chunks, in a box.

00:14:20.879 --> 00:14:25.490
These are also called vector machines.

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:32.690
And today, actually, GPUs more or less falls
into this category.

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:37.610
So these are single instruction, multiple
data machines.

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:41.759
So what's the difference between these two
architectures?

00:14:41.759 --> 00:14:47.290
On the left side we are showing a GPU and
on the right

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:49.699
is a CPU.

00:14:49.699 --> 00:14:58.519
Current CPUs are designed for latency.

00:14:58.519 --> 00:15:05.800
So they operate in-- well, single core of
CPU operates on single data elements.

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:14.440
And it's really designed to give fast response
to single problem.

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:18.879
On the other side, GPUs are really optimized
for throughput.

00:15:18.879 --> 00:15:23.720
So where the difference comes from in the
chip design actually.

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:27.009
What makes chip more complicated is the control
flow.

00:15:27.009 --> 00:15:32.910
If you want your chip to do different things,
the control flow becomes more complicated.

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:40.259
So GPUs takes advantage of that and have a
single control unit but multiple arithmetic

00:15:40.259 --> 00:15:43.079
units that they can do the operation.

00:15:43.079 --> 00:15:49.399
So with that they can produce a higher throughput
and they can actually be more energy efficient

00:15:49.399 --> 00:15:52.300
with that as well.

00:15:52.300 --> 00:16:00.600
So, but if you come to today's world where
we actually have architectures which have

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:05.160
multiple processors operating on different
data elements.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:11.570
And they can be actually in different forms.

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:15.910
One of them is the shared memory view of the
architectures.

00:16:15.910 --> 00:16:18.850
We used to call them SMPs-- symmetric multi-processors.

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:23.750
In that case, you have multiple processors.

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:30.580
They can all access to single memory, same
memory, but they can do independent work.

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:36.939
This will be your workstation with multi-cores
or the multiprocessors in it.

00:16:36.939 --> 00:16:44.170
Then we can go one step further, which handles
have processors connected through it and network.

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:50.509
This will be gigabit network or much faster
networks like InfiniBand.

00:16:50.509 --> 00:16:57.350
In that case, each person will have a local
direct access to its memory, but if they need

00:16:57.350 --> 00:17:01.730
to do something together, they need to communicate
over network.

00:17:01.730 --> 00:17:07.010
And, of course, we can have the hybrid model
that there are multiple processors.

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:12.970
Or today's, actually, we have the really--
sorry for the typo.

00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:14.320
Heterogeneous.

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:22.520
Computers which have different types of processing
units and each computing node and they are

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:25.030
connected through a network.

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:30.610
So let me just very quickly go over supercomputer.

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:35.360
So this is an HPC lecture, so I need to talk
about supercomputers.

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:42.200
So I'm going to start with a code attributes,
Seymour Cray of the Cray computers.

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:46.520
If you were plowing a field, which would you
rather use?

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:50.390
Two strong oxens or 1,024 chickens?

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:53.270
So, as you can see, this comes from a computer
scientist.

00:17:53.270 --> 00:17:54.270
It doesn't say 1,000.

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:58.120
It says 1,024 chickens.

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:01.170
And the answer to this question is not that
easy, actually.

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:03.540
It really depends what you want to do.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:09.550
If you don't need to plow really deep and
if you have a good mechanism to control those

00:18:09.550 --> 00:18:15.980
chickens, you can actually plow larger field
with 1,024 chickens, right?

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:20.900
But if you need to plow deep, you really need
to use two strong oxens.

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:28.310
And today's supercomputers are composed of
different architectures that are a mixture

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:29.920
of these two dilemmas.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:34.280
So on one side you have very powerful processing
elements.

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:37.640
They are connected to each other with fast
networks.

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:46.720
On the other side, you have computing elements
that are not that powerful by themselves,

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:53.590
like a single computing
element in the GPU may be, but as they're

00:18:53.590 --> 00:18:56.210
combined together, they are much more powerful.

00:18:56.210 --> 00:19:04.840
Top 500 is a website that lists world's fastest
supercomputers that are known to us.

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:09.460
At MIT, of course, other computers that we
are not aware of.

00:19:09.460 --> 00:19:17.320
These are basically ranked by some application,
some linear algebra solution to some linear

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:18.900
algebra problem.

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:27.010
So people argue that this is not a good representation
of the old application portfolio.

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:31.570
But, nevertheless, this is one of the lists.

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:39.270
And what I want to show here is just look
at the number of cores column here.

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:44.210
Today we're talking about 10 million cores.

00:19:44.210 --> 00:19:54.650
So if you want to really get high performance
on such a supercomputer, really need to think

00:19:54.650 --> 00:20:00.730
about massively parallelizing your computation.

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:06.450
At the bottom, you can see a chart that's
plotted from the beginning of the Top 500

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:10.180
list is about 1993, today.

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:14.540
And one shows the number one in this list.

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:24.360
As you can see, we are reaching about 100
petaflops and the goal to reach the exa scale,

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:28.180
exaflops, initial goal was 2018.

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:31.340
Now we're pushing about 2021.

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:34.690
And we're making progress in that.

00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:40.150
What I want you to look at-- take, for example,
one of the horizontal lines here.

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:41.170
Let's say 10 teraflops.

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:49.320
The number one machine was about in 2001,
2002, was 10 teraflops.

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:58.380
And in 2008, the bottom of the list, just
six years after, had that power.

00:20:58.380 --> 00:21:06.000
So about six to eight years number one machine
becomes number 500.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:14.140
And if you actually add another six to 10
years and six to eight, six to 10 years, that

00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:18.960
number 500 machine power, you'll have it in
your desktop.

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:27.490
So between about 14, 15 years, you may have
a computer that was as fast as world's fastest

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:29.570
computer 15 years ago.

00:21:29.570 --> 00:21:33.970
Another thing that I want to highlight is
your number one is really clearly separated

00:21:33.970 --> 00:21:35.040
from the rest.

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:39.711
If you look at the sum of power, the difference
between number one and sum and number one

00:21:39.711 --> 00:21:44.670
and number 500.

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:46.750
Number one is closer to the sum.

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:50.690
So, really, this is a top-heavy list.

00:21:50.690 --> 00:21:56.100
So very quickly-- I don't want to go too much
detail-- this is the progress of type of the

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:57.800
architectures we have.

00:21:57.800 --> 00:22:01.510
Initially there were signal processors that
were fastest machines.

00:22:01.510 --> 00:22:10.470
But if you just look at this decade, you'll
see that it's basically two types of computers.

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:17.820
Basically, one is clusters that are built
on commodity hardware, similar to the way

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:18.850
most of your workstations.

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:24.340
They are just connected with some little bit
faster networking flilibands and variants.

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:35.180
Or there are some, about 20%, really, designed
as supercomputers like IBM Blue Gene or the

00:22:35.180 --> 00:22:38.800
number one machine in our current architecture.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:49.530
Chip technology is also-- we will see that
commodity hardwares are becoming more prevalent.

00:22:49.530 --> 00:22:59.790
What I want to highlight here is, as I said
earlier, since 2003 we are using mostly multi-core

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:00.790
architectures.

00:23:00.790 --> 00:23:07.050
But starting from 2010, so beginning of this
decade, you will see what I call attack of

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:08.130
many cores.

00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:15.540
Those are GPUs, [INAUDIBLE], or those kind
of accelerators that I have, really those

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:19.920
1024 [INAUDIBLE] type of processors, the [? SAMD
?] processors,

00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:26.180
which, by themselves, they are not, individually
they're not powerful, but combined together

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:28.740
that they become much more powerful.

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:35.800
And as you can see, almost one fifth of the
Top500 machines have those architectures.

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:41.730
So, now, I want to change the topic.

00:23:41.730 --> 00:23:48.340
So I gave you a brief overview of what the
processors look like, how they're really composed,

00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:51.460
and what the current supercomputers look like.

00:23:51.460 --> 00:23:56.750
And now I want to look at from the application
perspective.

00:23:56.750 --> 00:23:59.320
So let's say that you have an application.

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:02.750
And you want to speed up that application.

00:24:02.750 --> 00:24:07.850
When I talk about application, I will use
the term workflow interchangeably.

00:24:07.850 --> 00:24:10.900
So there was another talk two weeks ago.

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:15.760
[? Igor Romy ?] actually explained workflows
wonderfully.

00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:22.250
So there's the workflows were at the higher
level [? now, ?] but here what I'm talking

00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:26.640
about will, workflow is an application itself.

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:32.250
So we'll look at parts of the workflow and
we'll try to figure out how we can make your

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:34.830
application, your workflow, faster.

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:43.180
The first thing that I will say, and those
three rules actually, they are really, if

00:24:43.180 --> 00:24:49.860
you look at the more common sense rules, but
we tend to overlook them, so I want to emphasize

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:50.860
a little bit.

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:56.010
So I have some questions [? on ?] the slide,
but just ignore the equations [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:57.840
for timing, just look at the bottom.

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:06.710
So let's say that one on the left, the bars,
blue and green bars, shows my application.

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:13.010
Let's say that I notice that on the green
part is something that's I'm doing repetitively

00:25:13.010 --> 00:25:19.760
and I can do much faster, or it's running
on one processor that I can actually get a

00:25:19.760 --> 00:25:21.730
faster processor for that.

00:25:21.730 --> 00:25:28.580
And let's say I'm improving that and I'm getting
what's viewed on the right hand side.

00:25:28.580 --> 00:25:34.050
So the question is, what's the speed up I
can get?

00:25:34.050 --> 00:25:42.120
So you can play with the arithmetic at the
top, and you'll see that new execution time

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:48.100
is, of course, the blue parts, which I haven't
improved.

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:49.540
They will stay.

00:25:49.540 --> 00:25:52.820
And I'm just improving the green part.

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:59.890
And what I would [INAUDIBLE] to the improvement
is that that will actually be limiting factor

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:01.100
for my speedup.

00:26:01.100 --> 00:26:08.070
Actually, another way of saying this, let's
say that I have infinite resources and I improve

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:12.790
that green part so that it's practically zero.

00:26:12.790 --> 00:26:21.160
Then the maximum speed up is 1 divided by
1 minus the fraction that I improved, which

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:25.560
is that [? parenthesis ?] says, whatever left
is in the blue.

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:27.840
So what does it mean?

00:26:27.840 --> 00:26:33.940
Let's say that you have a sequence analysis
pipeline, and you have one slow step.

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:36.010
So you call it slow.

00:26:36.010 --> 00:26:41.870
And it does error correction, for example,
of the input reads.

00:26:41.870 --> 00:26:43.610
So you want to do it fast.

00:26:43.610 --> 00:26:51.810
And someone says that, hey, there's a CPU
that's 10 times faster to do those kind of

00:26:51.810 --> 00:26:52.970
things.

00:26:52.970 --> 00:26:54.500
And that sounds very appealing.

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:57.270
You want to definitely use it.

00:26:57.270 --> 00:27:02.290
But when you look at the application, you
realize that applications actually I/O bound,

00:27:02.290 --> 00:27:06.740
meaning that it's reading
and writing the file 60% of time, only 40%

00:27:06.740 --> 00:27:09.590
of time is really doing that computation.

00:27:09.590 --> 00:27:15.280
So if you just plug the numbers here, what
you will see that your speed up will be it

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:17.130
was 1.56.

00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:20.530
So we were talking about a CPU that's 10 times
faster, right?

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:26.850
We are really attracted to those numbers because
they are big, but we lost the perspective

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:32.350
that this will actually only give us 1.6 time
speed up.

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:39.630
So another application that we have done in
my group, a long time ago actually, is multiple

00:27:39.630 --> 00:27:40.730
sequence alignments.

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:47.730
So this is a combinatorial problem, finding
the optimal solution, optimum in the sense

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:54.300
of computational, not biological, because
in the computers, we have to have a function

00:27:54.300 --> 00:27:56.470
to define optimality.

00:27:56.470 --> 00:28:02.990
And using that, if we actually use an optimal
algorithm, which will basically enumerate

00:28:02.990 --> 00:28:08.880
all possible solutions, even if it's a small
number of sequences, this may take hours.

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:12.910
And if you add a few more sequences, this
may take years, right?

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:20.040
Some of the problems will be intractable,
so we cannot solve them optimally, but we

00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:26.370
have good algorithms, heuristic algorithms
which will solve them in a faster time.

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:28.280
And Clustal W was one of them.

00:28:28.280 --> 00:28:32.840
So I'm not going to go into details of the
algorithm, but what I want to say is algorithm

00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:38.960
may basically have three phases, pairwise
alignment, guide tree computation, and progressive

00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:40.160
alignment.

00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:46.240
And when we actually look at the execution
time of the algorithm, if you look at that

00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:51.990
chart on the top left, we notice that pairwise
alignment was really taking quite a bit of

00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:54.060
time, depending on how many sequences you
have.

00:28:54.060 --> 00:28:59.270
And by [? increasing ?] the sequences, the
guide tree also becoming, taking too much

00:28:59.270 --> 00:29:00.270
time.

00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:05.530
But for most of our application case it was
about 400, so it was taking more than 90%

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:06.570
of the time.

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:07.900
So we thought, it's great.

00:29:07.900 --> 00:29:11.040
We met that [? part ?] parallel.

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:12.280
And as we did.

00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:19.600
So if you look at the chart on the right bottom,
so the green line shows the ideal speed up

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:24.650
you can get with preprocessors, if you have
drawing preprocessors, what you expect if

00:29:24.650 --> 00:29:27.750
your computation get p times faster.

00:29:27.750 --> 00:29:33.640
And as you can see, a pairwise alignment phase
of the algorithm, actually, with [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:33.640 --> 00:29:34.950
linear speed up.

00:29:34.950 --> 00:29:36.510
That's fantastic.

00:29:36.510 --> 00:29:45.040
But again, going back to Amdahl's law, plugging
this into the question and looking at, in

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:51.010
total, what was our speed up, you will see
that the speed up was just three, using 8

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:52.370
processors.

00:29:52.370 --> 00:30:00.230
So just again, to highlight, emphasize this
a little bit more, so this is just plot of

00:30:00.230 --> 00:30:08.720
if 10% of your code is sequential, that you
cannot parallelize what's the maximum speed

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:10.050
up you can get?

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:14.380
And on the x-axis I have from zero to 8,000
processors.

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:19.200
And you don't see that line because underneath
of all of them, and as you can see, it's really

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:23.140
actually less than 10.

00:30:23.140 --> 00:30:32.820
If you look at the best line I plotted to
here, it says if you have 1/10 of 1%, so 1,000

00:30:32.820 --> 00:30:37.510
of your program was sequential, so it can't
parallelize that part, but you parallelize

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:40.560
all of the rest, that's the speed up you will
get.

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:44.510
As you can see, you get pretty decent speed
up, up to 1,000 or so.

00:30:44.510 --> 00:30:46.890
But after that it starts letting out.

00:30:46.890 --> 00:30:51.850
And even if [? it ?] [? through ?] 8,000 processors,
you only get 900 speedup, right?

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:58.700
So you're practically wasting 7,000 processors
at this point.

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:04.280
So this is one thing you really need to pay
attention when you want to make your code

00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:05.280
faster.

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:13.160
So, and I don't think that I would say that
you need to pay attention, is pay attention

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:15.360
to locality.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:27.770
So like many years, our computers are composed
of memory hierarchies, such that no one in

00:31:27.770 --> 00:31:35.630
the hierarchy has a larger space, but their
speed is slower.

00:31:35.630 --> 00:31:43.590
So from CPU, to cache, to memory, to disk,
as you go down, you will get a larger and

00:31:43.590 --> 00:31:49.750
larger space, but access time becomes really
much slower and slower.

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:57.060
So what this tells us, really, if you can
structure your computation such that it actually

00:31:57.060 --> 00:32:05.120
fits into memory at the upper levels, you
will actually speed up your computation really,

00:32:05.120 --> 00:32:06.990
really fast.

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:15.160
So you really need to pay attention to this,
and at least think about how we can reorder

00:32:15.160 --> 00:32:20.530
the computation, such that you can keep the
data most of the time in memory, or most of

00:32:20.530 --> 00:32:26.090
the time in cache, if you can actually do
that.

00:32:26.090 --> 00:32:37.050
And another point in the pay attention to
locality is in some applications, this was

00:32:37.050 --> 00:32:43.320
basically a visualization application we have
done, again quite a bit of time ago, you may

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:46.730
have the data actually in distributed settings.

00:32:46.730 --> 00:32:50.730
So you may have, your [? collaborator ?] may
have been, for example, generating some data.

00:32:50.730 --> 00:33:03.430
In this particular case, we were looking at
different acquisition models for image modalities.

00:33:03.430 --> 00:33:11.310
And you want to create a visualization that
integrates all of this data.

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:13.850
The data sits in a remote location.

00:33:13.850 --> 00:33:17.580
You have some computing power, which you can
do the rendering.

00:33:17.580 --> 00:33:21.350
But your work station is not connected to
any of those.

00:33:21.350 --> 00:33:30.110
So obviously, the one thing you can do, you
can transfer all of the data to a visualization

00:33:30.110 --> 00:33:31.110
center.

00:33:31.110 --> 00:33:37.331
But that may require quite a bit of time depending
on the network they have in between.

00:33:37.331 --> 00:33:42.990
So instead, what you should be doing is you
really need to look at what user wants to

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:54.640
visualize, extract that data from the storage,
pass that to your rendering servers.

00:33:54.640 --> 00:34:01.180
Once they actually render, then you can get
to the client.

00:34:01.180 --> 00:34:08.780
So basically, the point here is instead of
moving the data to computation, you should

00:34:08.780 --> 00:34:11.450
really move the computation to data.

00:34:11.450 --> 00:34:18.049
In this case, simple indexing, filtering,
and creating the appropriate data structure.

00:34:18.049 --> 00:34:21.609
Move to where data [? originally ?] stored.

00:34:21.609 --> 00:34:26.450
Then we only move the data that we'll require
for our rendering.

00:34:26.450 --> 00:34:34.389
And then from there, we only move to the client
just a single image to display on the computer.

00:34:34.389 --> 00:34:41.419
So that's the second thing you need to really
pay attention to look out in.

00:34:41.419 --> 00:34:49.089
And the third and the most difficult one is
maybe is the take of advantage of parallelism.

00:34:49.089 --> 00:34:53.379
So I have different types of applications
here.

00:34:53.379 --> 00:35:01.270
And I have one biological application, well,
three classes that I will describe.

00:35:01.270 --> 00:35:08.349
I will broadly categorize the applications
into three classes.

00:35:08.349 --> 00:35:11.730
One of them is what I call bag-of-tasks model.

00:35:11.730 --> 00:35:17.650
So in that case, you have some computation
that you need to do that's they are independent

00:35:17.650 --> 00:35:22.329
of each other, but they may depend on some
input files.

00:35:22.329 --> 00:35:34.630
And DCMRI was one of the applications we actually
used, developed.

00:35:34.630 --> 00:35:36.420
And that fits into this model.

00:35:36.420 --> 00:35:38.520
So you have the tasks.

00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:40.600
And they are dependent on some files.

00:35:40.600 --> 00:35:45.670
And you can just independently parallelize
those to get efficiency.

00:35:45.670 --> 00:35:53.260
The second set of applications, which I'll
call non-streaming workflows in general--

00:35:53.260 --> 00:36:04.940
so, for example, [? general ?] mapping and
alignment is one of them.

00:36:04.940 --> 00:36:07.360
In that case, you have a workflow.

00:36:07.360 --> 00:36:11.720
So it could be simple pipeline, or it could
be a [INAUDIBLE] cyclic graph.

00:36:11.720 --> 00:36:14.910
Part of it maybe you can do parallel, but
you need to do first task.

00:36:14.910 --> 00:36:20.240
Then you can do the second, and third task
in the second level, and so on and so forth.

00:36:20.240 --> 00:36:25.900
And the third class of the application is
you still have a workflow, but you have multiple

00:36:25.900 --> 00:36:31.789
data elements that you can go through that
workflow.

00:36:31.789 --> 00:36:35.519
I'll call them streaming applications.

00:36:35.519 --> 00:36:36.670
OK?

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:42.230
And each of these applications categories
gives you a different parallelism.

00:36:42.230 --> 00:36:50.039
For example, in bag-of-tasks model, so you
can take these tasks and give each of these

00:36:50.039 --> 00:36:52.079
tasks to different processors.

00:36:52.079 --> 00:36:53.079
OK?

00:36:53.079 --> 00:36:59.770
So obviously, if you have more tasks than
processors, you can do multiple tasks to one

00:36:59.770 --> 00:37:00.770
processor.

00:37:00.770 --> 00:37:04.410
In that case, you again need to pay attention
to the locality.

00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:09.480
If you have multiple tasks that using the
same set of input files, then you put them

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:11.920
in the same processor.

00:37:11.920 --> 00:37:21.220
And you want to make sure that the total execution
time is minimized by giving same amount of

00:37:21.220 --> 00:37:23.559
work to everyone else.

00:37:23.559 --> 00:37:26.140
So I'll call this as a task parallelism.

00:37:26.140 --> 00:37:27.359
OK?

00:37:27.359 --> 00:37:31.089
The second one is non-streaming workflow.

00:37:31.089 --> 00:37:36.250
So in that case, let's say that we finished
the first phase and first task there, and

00:37:36.250 --> 00:37:37.259
we're doing the second.

00:37:37.259 --> 00:37:39.380
We are in the second level.

00:37:39.380 --> 00:37:43.940
We can actually do those two tasks parallel
to each other.

00:37:43.940 --> 00:37:50.390
And we can also maybe take advantage of one
more level of parallelism, data parallelism

00:37:50.390 --> 00:37:53.980
to do one of the heavy tasks that can be parallelized.

00:37:53.980 --> 00:37:56.920
So we distribute that into multiple processors.

00:37:56.920 --> 00:37:58.780
We do the data parallelism.

00:37:58.780 --> 00:38:04.569
And we can do the task parallelism by running
two different tasks concurrently on the pilot

00:38:04.569 --> 00:38:05.809
machine.

00:38:05.809 --> 00:38:07.049
OK?

00:38:07.049 --> 00:38:15.080
And the last one is streaming workflow, which
basically give us one more parallelism.

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:23.420
Once you finish the very first task, processing
of the very first elemental by way of first

00:38:23.420 --> 00:38:33.059
task, then you are ready to process them in
the second level.

00:38:33.059 --> 00:38:42.240
And you can do that concurrently with new
data elements, starting from the first task.

00:38:42.240 --> 00:38:45.750
So you can basically pipeline the execution.

00:38:45.750 --> 00:38:50.460
That way you can actually really reduce to
execution time nicely.

00:38:50.460 --> 00:38:51.960
You can still use data parallelism.

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:54.820
You can still use task parallelism.

00:38:54.820 --> 00:39:02.829
So the important thing for you to look at
is your application and figure out which of

00:39:02.829 --> 00:39:08.980
these types of parallelism is available for
your application.

00:39:08.980 --> 00:39:10.279
OK?

00:39:10.279 --> 00:39:17.900
So I'm going to use whole-slide to put those
image analysis for neuroblastoma.

00:39:17.900 --> 00:39:27.319
This is an application that we
developed with multiple collaborators, including

00:39:27.319 --> 00:39:37.599
Dr. [? Shimada ?] from Los Angeles and my
old friend, good old friend, Dr. [? Mettinger,

00:39:37.599 --> 00:39:43.549
?] who developed image analysis algorithms.

00:39:43.549 --> 00:39:51.460
The process basically mimics what a pathologist
is doing when they are [? reviewing ?] the

00:39:51.460 --> 00:39:52.460
whole-slides.

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:57.349
So it starts with the lower resolution of
the image.

00:39:57.349 --> 00:40:02.690
And if a simple diagnosis can be done at that
level, it's done.

00:40:02.690 --> 00:40:06.500
If not, then you will go into select some
regions.

00:40:06.500 --> 00:40:17.170
And you'll go into higher-level resolutions
and try to make the diagnosis based on the

00:40:17.170 --> 00:40:19.760
image which [? are ?] there.

00:40:19.760 --> 00:40:26.880
And the workflow for this is given at the
bottom, so you have multiple steps starting

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:34.740
from color conversion to some physical features,
looking at histograms and classifications.

00:40:34.740 --> 00:40:43.060
And if you cannot classify, you go back and
go one level higher resolution.

00:40:43.060 --> 00:40:45.849
So just it's a busy slide.

00:40:45.849 --> 00:40:50.910
But the point of this slide is, why do we
need to use HPC?

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:52.950
We're talking about these size pathology slides.

00:40:52.950 --> 00:40:59.700
These can be 120,000 by 120,000 pixels, [? 40
?] gigabytes of memory.

00:40:59.700 --> 00:41:06.839
And most of the time, our collaborators work
their algorithms in MATLAB.

00:41:06.839 --> 00:41:13.480
And if you just look at one small tile of
this image, like 1,000 by 1,000 pixels, it

00:41:13.480 --> 00:41:16.259
will takes about 6 seconds in MATLAB.

00:41:16.259 --> 00:41:23.079
If you convert that into more efficient programming
with C, you can get to less than a second.

00:41:23.079 --> 00:41:30.390
But even with that, even for a small slide
and half of the background, it will take about

00:41:30.390 --> 00:41:33.069
20 minutes to actually analyze the slide.

00:41:33.069 --> 00:41:42.140
So what we really want to do to make this
practical, useful in the clinical setting,

00:41:42.140 --> 00:41:49.460
we needed to get this under couple of seconds,
because you may really need to look at multiple

00:41:49.460 --> 00:41:52.339
slides per patient.

00:41:52.339 --> 00:42:01.329
And basically, processing this, which just
current CP algorithms will not scale.

00:42:01.329 --> 00:42:05.680
You will need more than a year to process
one year worth of data.

00:42:05.680 --> 00:42:08.960
So how do we do that?

00:42:08.960 --> 00:42:18.329
So first of all, we can chop the big image,
the slide, into small tiles.

00:42:18.329 --> 00:42:24.309
When I say small, maybe 1,000 by 1,000 or
4,000 by 4,000 pixels.

00:42:24.309 --> 00:42:25.839
On the left side is one slide.

00:42:25.839 --> 00:42:30.059
And each of those small boxes is one tile.

00:42:30.059 --> 00:42:34.400
Then we can give those tiles to computational
units.

00:42:34.400 --> 00:42:43.160
We can have multiple computational units,
CPUs, GPUs, and [INAUDIBLE] files.

00:42:43.160 --> 00:42:50.250
And we want to process them through them and
get the classification out of it.

00:42:50.250 --> 00:42:56.099
So this application actually provides all
of those three levels of parallelism I mentioned.

00:42:56.099 --> 00:43:00.130
So first of all, if you look at the workflow,
there were different steps.

00:43:00.130 --> 00:43:02.009
So there were different tasks.

00:43:02.009 --> 00:43:08.359
So I can do them concurrently, and I can pipeline
them as well.

00:43:08.359 --> 00:43:12.880
What I can also do, I can take advantage of
data parallelism.

00:43:12.880 --> 00:43:22.849
Here, more deeply, this is an application
that some of the things that I'm computing

00:43:22.849 --> 00:43:30.619
fits very well to GPU's scheme of computing,
basically single instruction, multiple data.

00:43:30.619 --> 00:43:37.210
So what I'm showing here is, again, too much
information this slide.

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:44.820
But just if you look at the top row, there
are different tasks in my workflow.

00:43:44.820 --> 00:43:51.500
And we list how many-- what size of input
and output is producing.

00:43:51.500 --> 00:43:56.250
Is it
computationally heavy or not?

00:43:56.250 --> 00:43:57.839
Is there locality?

00:43:57.839 --> 00:44:04.280
Is it accessing the same data or not?

00:44:04.280 --> 00:44:07.619
And all of them have different characteristics.

00:44:07.619 --> 00:44:13.750
And we have implemented the GPU version of
all of those phases, and what we have seen,

00:44:13.750 --> 00:44:17.099
really, each one of them gives different speedup,
right?

00:44:17.099 --> 00:44:22.539
The speed up, with respect to a single CPU
core, which I will tell that that's usually

00:44:22.539 --> 00:44:25.259
not a good way of representing numbers.

00:44:25.259 --> 00:44:30.690
But just to be able to compare those for having
a single baseline is good.

00:44:30.690 --> 00:44:35.319
Speedup can be from 166 to eight times.

00:44:35.319 --> 00:44:43.150
So since your CPU also have multiple cores,
you can actually do multiple of them.

00:44:43.150 --> 00:44:48.960
So if you have a CPU with eight cores, really,
doing the histogram computation in GPU doesn't

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:54.160
make sense, because it's not really giving
you a faster execution time.

00:44:54.160 --> 00:44:59.029
Yet another [? basic ?] slide.

00:44:59.029 --> 00:45:05.670
Title recalculation rate means the times that
we call the higher resolution.

00:45:05.670 --> 00:45:10.180
So as that increases, we need to process bigger
and bigger tiles.

00:45:10.180 --> 00:45:18.540
If you just look at the bottom top-left chart
and the bottom white bars, that's how much

00:45:18.540 --> 00:45:26.130
time it takes-- how much speedup I get with
using GPU only processing.

00:45:26.130 --> 00:45:33.779
On top of it, I have some workflow optimizations
which includes how to efficiently use pipelining

00:45:33.779 --> 00:45:41.869
and overlapping computations and communications,
which we don't need to go into detail.

00:45:41.869 --> 00:45:50.920
As the tile gets bigger, doing it in GPU obviously
gets bigger, because you have more data elements

00:45:50.920 --> 00:45:53.880
to process to do the same computation.

00:45:53.880 --> 00:45:56.740
So this fits to GPU very well.

00:45:56.740 --> 00:46:02.609
But what I want to highlight at the bottom,
if you look at the chart on the right, this

00:46:02.609 --> 00:46:04.599
is actually the same thing.

00:46:04.599 --> 00:46:09.059
But what we are adding is additional CPU processors.

00:46:09.059 --> 00:46:13.289
They don't have GPU.

00:46:13.289 --> 00:46:14.990
So we have a heterogeneous environment.

00:46:14.990 --> 00:46:17.520
So we are just adding CPUs.

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:21.170
So we are adding same number of CPUs.

00:46:21.170 --> 00:46:25.030
So that means one may expect to get two times
speedup.

00:46:25.030 --> 00:46:28.190
We actually get three times speedup.

00:46:28.190 --> 00:46:30.769
So how is that possible?

00:46:30.769 --> 00:46:34.980
It's possible because we were not efficiently
utilizing GPUs.

00:46:34.980 --> 00:46:43.859
Some of the computations like processing the
small tiles, they are really not good for

00:46:43.859 --> 00:46:48.349
doing those kind of computations in GPU.

00:46:48.349 --> 00:46:54.711
To get the best out of GPU, you really need
to process a larger amount of data, and you

00:46:54.711 --> 00:46:59.180
should be doing the same type of work.

00:46:59.180 --> 00:47:03.640
So I guess I've run really, really fast.

00:47:03.640 --> 00:47:10.130
So that wasn't really my intention, but I
think I may have more time for more questions,

00:47:10.130 --> 00:47:11.490
I guess.

00:47:11.490 --> 00:47:17.009
So I talked about really high-performance
computing until now, and my title says distributed

00:47:17.009 --> 00:47:18.380
computing, cloud computing.

00:47:18.380 --> 00:47:20.529
What about cloud computing?

00:47:20.529 --> 00:47:27.050
So let me just take a few minutes of time
and talk about cloud computing.

00:47:27.050 --> 00:47:35.460
So even though names has clouds-- I guess
everyone in this talk knows that it's not

00:47:35.460 --> 00:47:36.730
really in the cloud.

00:47:36.730 --> 00:47:38.750
It's someone else's computer, right?

00:47:38.750 --> 00:47:45.400
So it's nice that there are many companies
and agencies that are providing us these as

00:47:45.400 --> 00:47:46.849
resources.

00:47:46.849 --> 00:47:54.630
And in many cases, these are offered in case
of Amazon cloud and Microsoft cases.

00:47:54.630 --> 00:48:01.950
These are offered by companies, and they are
models for-- you rent your computing power

00:48:01.950 --> 00:48:04.799
instead of buying that computing power.

00:48:04.799 --> 00:48:14.190
Which is great, if you don't need the computation
24/7 in your lab, and you have times that

00:48:14.190 --> 00:48:16.070
you actually need to analyze the data.

00:48:16.070 --> 00:48:21.559
And you need to just-- during that time, if
you can have access, that's great.

00:48:21.559 --> 00:48:27.630
And in that case, cloud computing is really
helpful.

00:48:27.630 --> 00:48:33.319
But one thing you really need to pay attention,
you really need to pay for computer data storage

00:48:33.319 --> 00:48:35.740
and transfer, right?

00:48:35.740 --> 00:48:43.400
So all of these actually comes back to my
golden rules that I told you.

00:48:43.400 --> 00:48:45.829
You're paying for the compute time.

00:48:45.829 --> 00:48:50.009
So you need to make sure that you're efficiently
using computational time, so that you're not

00:48:50.009 --> 00:48:51.819
wasting your computational time.

00:48:51.819 --> 00:48:58.410
You're going to pay for data storage, and
of course, [INAUDIBLE] data, but you'll be

00:48:58.410 --> 00:49:01.079
also paying for the data transfer.

00:49:01.079 --> 00:49:06.500
So minimizing the data transfer will help
you to save money as well.

00:49:06.500 --> 00:49:10.010
So I don't want to sound negative about it.

00:49:10.010 --> 00:49:15.660
In contrast, I'm extremely happy that we have
this infrastructure.

00:49:15.660 --> 00:49:23.290
This is actually probably our best bet right
now to get sharing of the large data workflows

00:49:23.290 --> 00:49:27.770
and computational resources between multiple
institutions.

00:49:27.770 --> 00:49:34.549
So after many different iterations of earlier
distribute computing, grid computing standardization

00:49:34.549 --> 00:49:45.730
efforts, this is actually really one way that
we can finally get to share large data and

00:49:45.730 --> 00:49:48.069
workflows and work together.

00:49:48.069 --> 00:49:53.329
And also, for most of us, not everyone has
direct access to the supercomputers.

00:49:53.329 --> 00:49:59.650
This is really our best bet to get scalability
and speed.

00:49:59.650 --> 00:50:06.119
So because I don't really have time, I cannot
go into detail how you can really use this.

00:50:06.119 --> 00:50:10.410
But everything we're discussing really applies
to cloud computing as well.

00:50:10.410 --> 00:50:18.200
If you look at, for example, Amazon today,
they offer multiple virtual machines and instances

00:50:18.200 --> 00:50:20.950
with different number of cores.

00:50:20.950 --> 00:50:23.450
They even offer it with the GPUs.

00:50:23.450 --> 00:50:34.440
And Microsoft has also has started offering
the instances with the GPUs as well.

00:50:34.440 --> 00:50:42.819
I will just point to one of the review papers
that I used to give as a reading assignment

00:50:42.819 --> 00:50:47.299
to my students from the Nature [INAUDIBLE]
Genetics, even though I don't really agree

00:50:47.299 --> 00:50:52.019
for everything that's said there.

00:50:52.019 --> 00:50:56.170
To me, heterogeneous computing is not really
much different than cloud computing, because

00:50:56.170 --> 00:50:58.490
it's really converged to the same thing.

00:50:58.490 --> 00:51:06.069
Now, cloud computing has given the heterogeneous
compute platforms for us.

00:51:06.069 --> 00:51:12.739
It's a good review to looking at different
applications and how they may also map to

00:51:12.739 --> 00:51:14.369
some of those resources.

00:51:14.369 --> 00:51:22.980
What's more entertaining for me was to also
read the correspondences following this, too,

00:51:22.980 --> 00:51:29.809
this paper by groups [INAUDIBLE] and [INAUDIBLE]
the author's response.

00:51:29.809 --> 00:51:35.950
And they're arguing the suitability of the
cloud from different perspectives, which was

00:51:35.950 --> 00:51:42.869
interesting, because both of them were correct
in their arguments, but they were not really

00:51:42.869 --> 00:51:44.710
arguing the same thing.

00:51:44.710 --> 00:51:52.849
One was talking about transferring one petabyte
of data to every single node in the 1,000-node

00:51:52.849 --> 00:51:56.420
cloud environment, which will cost you $6
million, at that time.

00:51:56.420 --> 00:52:03.640
And the other was talking about just processing
six one petabyte data in 1,000 processing

00:52:03.640 --> 00:52:09.390
elements, which will cost just a couple of
thousand, which is much more manageable.

00:52:09.390 --> 00:52:14.190
The reason that I'm really highlighting this,
when you're talking about different infrastructures

00:52:14.190 --> 00:52:19.309
and different ways of parallelizing, make
sure that you're talking about parallelizing

00:52:19.309 --> 00:52:20.720
the same application.

00:52:20.720 --> 00:52:27.109
We are not talking about making the program
much larger than what it was doing initially.

00:52:27.109 --> 00:52:33.119
Make sure that you have the best [? sequential
?] [? per ?] program in place, so that you

00:52:33.119 --> 00:52:36.750
can actually focus on parallelizing it.

00:52:36.750 --> 00:52:40.661
So I will end up with a summary.

00:52:40.661 --> 00:52:42.280
I know I showed you too many things.

00:52:42.280 --> 00:52:52.349
But I really want you leave this presentation
with keeping in mind these three rules and

00:52:52.349 --> 00:52:54.680
what it can give you.

00:52:54.680 --> 00:52:59.010
I'm not expecting turn all of you into computer
scientists.

00:52:59.010 --> 00:53:05.269
And as last week [? Trey ?] highlighted, don't
try to be Jack of all trades.

00:53:05.269 --> 00:53:07.300
It's not good.

00:53:07.300 --> 00:53:14.400
The best thing is to find the right collaborator
to work which you for the right set of problems.

00:53:14.400 --> 00:53:21.019
This could be specialized biologist or this
could be a computer scientist like myself

00:53:21.019 --> 00:53:23.559
or others.

00:53:23.559 --> 00:53:29.630
But what I'm hoping that this lecture gave
you-- an overview when you think about your

00:53:29.630 --> 00:53:39.029
application, what are the places that you
need to look for making your application faster?

00:53:39.029 --> 00:53:46.380
And this will be a starting point discussion
with your computer scientist colleagues.

00:53:46.380 --> 00:53:52.660
So again, just quickly, the three things is--
one, we really need to focus what's the most

00:53:52.660 --> 00:53:53.739
time-consuming?

00:53:53.739 --> 00:53:55.049
What's the common case?

00:53:55.049 --> 00:53:57.869
But you also think about, how frequent that
is?

00:53:57.869 --> 00:54:04.020
If it's just the 50% of your computation,
even if you improve that infinitely, you'll

00:54:04.020 --> 00:54:05.680
only get two times speedup.

00:54:05.680 --> 00:54:10.829
So don't expect miracles from [? cloud or
?] parallel computing if you're only improving

00:54:10.829 --> 00:54:13.670
50% of your computation.

00:54:13.670 --> 00:54:17.859
The second is you really need to pay attention
to locality.

00:54:17.859 --> 00:54:26.430
And you need to bring the computation to closer
to your CPU when it's possible in single-site

00:54:26.430 --> 00:54:28.540
environments.

00:54:28.540 --> 00:54:32.609
But when it's not possible, if the data is
really big and you're talking about distributed

00:54:32.609 --> 00:54:37.169
environment, then you really think about moving
computation to [? data.

00:54:37.169 --> 00:54:39.520
?]
And the third thing is taking advantage of

00:54:39.520 --> 00:54:40.650
parallelism.

00:54:40.650 --> 00:54:47.200
So almost everything we do has many levels
of parallelism, including [? passed ?] data

00:54:47.200 --> 00:54:48.799
and pipeline parallelism.

00:54:48.799 --> 00:54:51.509
You really need to take advantage of those.

00:54:51.509 --> 00:54:57.099
Don't forget, again, the fastest processor
doesn't mean that your application run fast.

00:54:57.099 --> 00:55:00.049
You need to make sure that you find the most
suitable architecture.

00:55:00.049 --> 00:55:05.000
So for example, if you're doing a regular
computations like for the image analysis applications

00:55:05.000 --> 00:55:11.559
we have shown, the GPUs are great for them
as long as you process [? through ?] a big

00:55:11.559 --> 00:55:15.099
chunk of images.

00:55:15.099 --> 00:55:21.319
And when talking about GPUs or any other architectures,
you need to make sure that you have a fair

00:55:21.319 --> 00:55:23.549
comparison.

00:55:23.549 --> 00:55:29.099
Comparing your performance of whole GPU with
a single-core performance is not a good idea.

00:55:29.099 --> 00:55:33.039
What you need to be comparing is if you're
comparing a GPU, you really need to compare

00:55:33.039 --> 00:55:39.339
against what's the comparable current CPU
using multi-cores of that CPU.

00:55:39.339 --> 00:55:46.799
So in regular applications, I've seen GPUs
can be up to 10 times faster in some applications.

00:55:46.799 --> 00:55:51.180
But in real life, really, it's about three
to five times faster.

00:55:51.180 --> 00:55:53.029
It's not 100 times faster.

00:55:53.029 --> 00:55:58.990
But again, it really depends on your application
characteristics.

00:55:58.990 --> 00:56:05.430
So that's actually all I want to cover today.

00:56:05.430 --> 00:56:07.099
And that's [? half of ?] [? the answer.

00:56:07.099 --> 00:56:08.819
?]
That's wonderful, [INAUDIBLE].

00:56:08.819 --> 00:56:13.999
Thank you so much for that really comprehensive
overview of high performance and cloud computing

00:56:13.999 --> 00:56:20.010
and all the factors that go into the decisions
for how you begin the process of parallelizing

00:56:20.010 --> 00:56:23.239
your code and applying it.

00:56:23.239 --> 00:56:25.381
We have time for a couple of questions.

00:56:25.381 --> 00:56:32.099
The first question that we have is really
sort of a little more practical-- is what

00:56:32.099 --> 00:56:38.070
sort of rules of thumb or advice could you
give to people on when exactly to begin the

00:56:38.070 --> 00:56:47.039
process of deciding that their code is-- could
run faster or whatnot, and I guess in the

00:56:47.039 --> 00:56:53.990
context of, could I just improve the performance
of my code by writing better code or do I

00:56:53.990 --> 00:56:58.670
decide that, my code is as good as I'm going
to get it, now I should go and try and improve

00:56:58.670 --> 00:57:04.420
its speed by going to high-performance computing
solutions such as parallel-

00:57:04.420 --> 00:57:06.420
Thank you very much.

00:57:06.420 --> 00:57:09.180
This is a fantastic question.

00:57:09.180 --> 00:57:10.350
We see this all the time.

00:57:10.350 --> 00:57:13.730
And I tried to allude to this a little bit.

00:57:13.730 --> 00:57:15.750
But let me expand on that.

00:57:15.750 --> 00:57:25.599
So when to decide it's time to make your code
parallel-- the first thing you really need

00:57:25.599 --> 00:57:30.539
to make sure that-- you have the fastest sequential
code.

00:57:30.539 --> 00:57:36.599
So your code is most optimal in the sequential
setting before going to parallel.

00:57:36.599 --> 00:57:44.839
If you have a very inefficient sequential
code, so you can much easily parallelize that.

00:57:44.839 --> 00:57:49.359
It's much easier to parallelize that in many
cases.

00:57:49.359 --> 00:57:55.920
You can just [? embarrassingly, ?] or what
I guess-- pleasingly parallelize that by partitioning

00:57:55.920 --> 00:57:57.279
the work.

00:57:57.279 --> 00:58:06.390
But it is highly possible that there's a better
algorithm, there's a faster way of doing that

00:58:06.390 --> 00:58:07.599
in sequential setting.

00:58:07.599 --> 00:58:13.019
And that could be still much faster than any
parallel example.

00:58:13.019 --> 00:58:17.739
One example from-- I have had multiple examples
of this.

00:58:17.739 --> 00:58:23.349
We had a [INAUDIBLE] parallel algorithm to
do one

00:58:23.349 --> 00:58:25.840
specific task.

00:58:25.840 --> 00:58:27.970
And we couldn't get a good speed up.

00:58:27.970 --> 00:58:30.749
But it was a parallel algorithm.

00:58:30.749 --> 00:58:39.720
Later, another research team came up and they
said they have a framework which it will allow

00:58:39.720 --> 00:58:46.279
our parallel algorithm to scale wonderfully--
so linear speed-up.

00:58:46.279 --> 00:58:47.890
This fascinated me, of course.

00:58:47.890 --> 00:58:49.359
This is something I want.

00:58:49.359 --> 00:58:55.210
But when I look at it carefully, yes, their
framework had a good scaling up.

00:58:55.210 --> 00:59:04.440
But their sequential execution time was 1,000
times slower than my sequential time.

00:59:04.440 --> 00:59:08.710
So in that case, really wasting resources
is not going to help you.

00:59:08.710 --> 00:59:12.489
You need to make sure that you have fastest
code.

00:59:12.489 --> 00:59:20.819
But of course, when to stop debugging and
making sure that you have the fastest code--

00:59:20.819 --> 00:59:22.930
that's a very difficult question.

00:59:22.930 --> 00:59:32.510
So you need to look at, again, maybe 80/20
rule.

00:59:32.510 --> 00:59:38.170
You don't want to spend 80% of time to make
your code 20% faster.

00:59:38.170 --> 00:59:43.979
So you need to look in easy parts that you
can make it fast.

00:59:43.979 --> 00:59:45.609
That's very sound advice.

00:59:45.609 --> 00:59:50.819
One other question is what sort of technologies
do you see that are currently emerging or

00:59:50.819 --> 00:59:56.880
that are on the horizon that will help with
minimizing latency of data transfer so if

00:59:56.880 --> 01:00:04.239
you are moving data around, you're not hidebound
by data transfer rates as Internet 2 or whatever--

01:00:04.239 --> 01:00:07.009
Internet 3-- I don't know what's on the horizon.

01:00:07.009 --> 01:00:12.040
Are those technologies going to be helpful?

01:00:12.040 --> 01:00:16.650
There are some technologies, some network
technologies-- would be really helpful in

01:00:16.650 --> 01:00:18.529
the higher end right now.

01:00:18.529 --> 01:00:28.729
But I'm afraid we have to deal with latency
in our data life more often than we are doing

01:00:28.729 --> 01:00:29.729
right now.

01:00:29.729 --> 01:00:38.349
And so most of the time, an end user [INAUDIBLE]
is end up in [? workstation.

01:00:38.349 --> 01:00:46.450
?] And whatever your university or company's
network is limited, you will be limited with

01:00:46.450 --> 01:00:47.890
that.

01:00:47.890 --> 01:00:54.300
So InfiniBand, for example, is getting much
faster network.

01:00:54.300 --> 01:01:00.829
But if you look at, again, even the earlier
[? plots ?] I've showed you, the improvements

01:01:00.829 --> 01:01:04.710
are usually more in bandwidth than latency.

01:01:04.710 --> 01:01:10.180
So latency is improving, but at a much slower
pace.

01:01:10.180 --> 01:01:18.769
So you really need to get used to low latency
and think about how you can make your algorithm

01:01:18.769 --> 01:01:28.480
program faster by focusing on throughput and
maybe looking at parallelism overlapping so

01:01:28.480 --> 01:01:36.359
that you gave the illusion of low latency,
or basically focusing on memory hierarchy

01:01:36.359 --> 01:01:40.410
and using it better, again, to give low latency.

01:01:40.410 --> 01:01:48.650
So again, these-- the rules I laid out-- is
the best thing to do to achieve better latency.

01:01:48.650 --> 01:01:49.650
Fantastic.

01:01:49.650 --> 01:01:53.170
Well, I'm sorry, everybody who's been writing
in questions.

01:01:53.170 --> 01:01:54.700
We've run out of time this morning.

01:01:54.700 --> 01:02:00.569
But I want to-- hope you'll join me in thanking
Umit for giving us a wonderful talk this morning

01:02:00.569 --> 01:02:06.019
on modern computing cloud distributed in high
performance-- really, some great ideas and

01:02:06.019 --> 01:02:11.260
great heuristics there to use as you're thinking
about parallelizing your code.

01:02:11.260 --> 01:02:18.440
Please join us again next week, next Friday,
